# 2. The Blackwell information poset

Fix a finite parameter/state set \(X\).

Let \(\mathsf B_X\) be the set of finite statistical experiments/channels with source \(X\), modulo Blackwell equivalence.

Use the order

\[
E\le_B F
\]

to mean:

\[
F\text{ Blackwell-dominates }E.
\]

Thus smaller elements are less informative.

For \(E\in\mathsf B_X\), its principal lower set is

\[
\downarrow E
=
\{G\in\mathsf B_X:G\le_B E\}.
\]

Operationally, \(\downarrow E\) is the class of every finite experiment obtainable from \(E\) by post-processing.

Its \(k\)-output slice is exactly

\[
\boxed{
(\downarrow E)_k
=
\mathcal R_k(E),
}
\]

the reachable \(k\)-decision region defined in CW-001A.

So the full reachable-decision system is simply the principal Blackwell ideal viewed target-arity by target-arity.

---

# 3. MacNeille completion

For \(S\subseteq\mathsf B_X\), define

\[
U(S)
=
\{F:F\ge_B E\ \forall E\in S\},
\]

\[
L(S)
=
\{G:G\le_B E\ \forall E\in S\}.
\]

The Dedekind–MacNeille closure is

\[
\operatorname{cl}_{DM}(S)
=
L(U(S)).
\]

The MacNeille completion is

\[
\operatorname{DM}(\mathsf B_X)
=
\{
A\subseteq\mathsf B_X:
A=L(U(A))
\}.
\]

Ordered by inclusion, it is a complete lattice.

The canonical embedding is

\[
\boxed{
j(E)=\downarrow E.
}
\]

Classical MacNeille theory says this embedding preserves every meet and join that already exists in the original poset and is the minimal complete lattice completion in the standard join-dense/meet-dense sense.

---

# 4. Exact common information cut

Take experiments

\[
E_1,\ldots,E_P.
\]

Their common lower set is

\[
\boxed{
C(E_1,\ldots,E_P)
=
L(\{E_1,\ldots,E_P\})
=
\bigcap_{p=1}^P\downarrow E_p.
}
\]

Because every set of lower bounds \(L(S)\) is MacNeille-closed,

\[
C(E_1,\ldots,E_P)
\in
\operatorname{DM}(\mathsf B_X).
\]

### Theorem CW-001B.1 — The missing Blackwell meet always exists as a MacNeille cut

In the Dedekind–MacNeille completion,

\[
\boxed{
j(E_1)\wedge\cdots\wedge j(E_P)
=
\bigcap_p\downarrow E_p.
}
\]

Thus the exact common lower information object exists canonically even when no genuine experiment represents it.

### Proof

Meet in the MacNeille lattice is set intersection.

Each \(j(E_p)=\downarrow E_p\).

Therefore their meet is their intersection.

---

# 5. Genuine-meet criterion = principality

### Theorem CW-001B.2 — Blackwell meet iff the common cut is principal

The genuine Blackwell meet

\[
M=E_1\wedge_B\cdots\wedge_B E_P
\]

exists iff there is an experiment \(M\) such that

\[
\boxed{
C(E_1,\ldots,E_P)=\downarrow M.
}
\]

Equivalently:

\[
\boxed{
\text{Blackwell meet exists}
\iff
\text{the MacNeille common cut is principal}.
}
\]

### Proof

If \(M\) is a greatest common lower bound, the set of common lower bounds is exactly the principal ideal generated by \(M\).

Conversely, if the common lower set is \(\downarrow M\), then \(M\) belongs to it and dominates every other common lower bound, so \(M\) is the greatest common lower bound.

### Interpretation

The Blackwell non-lattice pathology is not absence of an exact common lower-information object.

It is:

\[
\boxed{
\text{nonprincipality of the canonical common-information cut}.
}
\]

This is a more precise order-theoretic statement than "the meet does not exist."

---

# 6. Decision regions are slices of the MacNeille cut

Let

\[
\mathsf B_{X,k}
\]

denote Blackwell classes having a representative with output alphabet \([k]\).

Then

\[
\boxed{
C_k^\cap
=
C(E_1,\ldots,E_P)
\cap
\mathsf B_{X,k}.
}
\]

In matrix form this is exactly

\[
\boxed{
C_k^\cap
=
\bigcap_p
\mathcal R_k(E_p).
}
\]

Therefore the coherent decision-system construction of CW-001A is the arity-resolved operational presentation of the MacNeille common cut.

This yields an exact hierarchy:

\[
\text{MacNeille cut}
\longrightarrow
\{
C_2^\cap,C_3^\cap,\ldots
\}.
\]

The cut is principal iff all these slices are jointly generated by one experiment.

