# 8. Bertschinger–Rauh = explicit nonprincipal MacNeille cut

Bertschinger and Rauh construct two 3-state channels \(\kappa_1,\kappa_2\) whose binary-decision zonotopes satisfy

\[
Z_{\kappa_1}\cap Z_{\kappa_2}
\]

is not a zonotope because it has triangular faces.

Their proof shows that if a greatest common lower bound \(\mu\) existed, its zonotope would have to equal the full intersection, which is impossible.

In MacNeille language:

\[
\boxed{
L(\{\kappa_1,\kappa_2\})
}
\]

is a concrete nonprincipal cut.

Thus their 2014 theorem supplies an explicit statistical witness that the canonical MacNeille completion strictly enlarges the genuine-experiment poset when \(|X|>2\).

For \(|X|=2\), their Proposition 16 says the Blackwell order already forms a lattice.

Hence binary-state common cuts generated by two experiments are principal.

