# 9. A new exact notion: principality defect

The existence question can now be refined.

For a common cut

\[
C=L(S),
\]

define its **maximal-boundary set**

\[
\operatorname{Max}(C)
=
\{
G\in C:
\nexists H\in C,\ G<_B H
\}.
\]

If \(C=\downarrow M\) is principal, then

\[
\operatorname{Max}(C)=\{M\}.
\]

But nonprincipality has more than one possible form:

1. multiple incomparable maximal common lower bounds;
2. infinitely many maximal common lower bounds;
3. no maximal element at all, only increasing approximating nets.

This suggests a hierarchy of meet failure stronger than the yes/no lattice question.

No novelty claim is made yet.

