# 10. Minimal completion versus operational/metric completion

Three different completions must now be separated.

## A. Dedekind–MacNeille completion

Purpose:
exact order completion.

Property:
smallest complete lattice containing the Blackwell poset in the standard MacNeille sense.

New elements:
cuts / nonprincipal common-information objects.

## B. Le Cam generalized experiments/transitions

Purpose:
remove measure-theoretic pathologies and obtain sufficiently general decision procedures/experiment representations.

Torgersen records that:
- ordinary randomizations can be inadequate;
- transitions give the fully general comparison statement;
- generalized variables repair failures of weak compactness;
- experiments can be represented through L/M spaces and conical measures.

This is an operational/functional-analytic enlargement.

## C. Deficiency metric completion/topology

Purpose:
quantitative approximation.

Torgersen records that:
- deficiency has equivalent risk/randomization meanings;
- deficiency distance is a natural sup-distance on experiment functionals;
- for finite parameter sets, standard/conical measure representations connect deficiency topology to weak convergence.

These three completions need not coincide.

### New forced question

\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{DM}(\mathsf B_X)
\quad\text{vs}\quad
\text{Le Cam generalized experiments}
\quad\text{vs}\quad
\overline{\mathsf B_X}^{\,\Delta}
}
\]

where the last object denotes a deficiency-metric completion/closure.

Which maps exist between them?

Which MacNeille cuts are representable by generalized experiments?

Which are limits in deficiency?

This is now the main CW-001 problem.

